# One fingerprint, pinned deliberately narrowly.
#
# `cf2cdd6` pasted an `LLMReplayMiss` traceback into the M6-A note verbatim. The
# error text ends `... key=<16 hex chars>`, and gitleaks' `generic-api-key` rule
# matched it. The value is the truncated sha256 that `llm/cache.py::make_key`
# computes over a PROMPT — the same digest that names the fixture files — not a
# credential. `cache.py` deliberately excludes the API key from that hash so
# recorded fixtures are safe to commit.
#
# VERIFIED, not assumed: the matched value is the prefix of a `key` field in a
# committed replay fixture, and the repository contains no configured
# GROQ/GEMINI/OPENROUTER key verbatim.
#
# WHY A FINGERPRINT AND NOT A REGEX. `.gitleaks.toml` already allowlists
# `^[0-9a-f]{64}$` — full sha256 cache keys — and records the mutation test that
# proved a wider allowlist hides real keys. The obvious move here, adding
# `^[0-9a-f]{16}$`, would silence every 64-bit hex string in the repository
# forever to excuse one line in one doc. A fingerprint is scoped to exactly one
# commit, file, rule and line, so it cannot hide anything else.
#
# HEAD no longer contains the string — the note now elides the digest — so this
# entry covers only the historical blob and should never need a sibling.
